  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>Philip or Phillip H. Newton is listed in <ref target="civic?id=Queensberry%20Street">Queensberry Street</ref> from 1878 to 1910, at No. 16 to 1885 and at No. 215 from 1890, after the street was renumbered. He is listed as an asphalter from 1900.</p>
        <p>Mrs Mary Newton is listed at No. 215 in 1915 and 1920, and Mrs A. J. Newton at No. 193 in 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>On the electoral roll</head>
        <p>Phillip Newton, asphalter, is on the 1905 <ref target="civic?id=Electoral-Rolls">electoral roll</ref> at 215 Queensberry Street.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>Charles Cover, bootmaker, is next door from 1879 to 1900, at No. 18 and then No. 213, which shows the renumbering. William Wright, grocer, is at No. 14 from 1878 to 1880. From 1885 the Independent Church and Mission Hall is on the other side, and from 1905 the house is listed on the <ref target="civic?id=Lansdowne%20Place">Lansdowne Place</ref> corner.</p>
      </div>
